1,2 $ Pileus not or hardiy hygrophanous, not or hardly translucently striate, usually very dark brown to almost black
1,6 $ Pileus distinct!y hygrophanous, translucently striate at 1east to halfway the radius, usually up to centre, medium brown, red-brown, grey-brown or blackish brown(at centre)
2,3 $ Spores large, frequent!y over 10 µm long; hymenophoral trama of short elements; clamp-connections present in ali tissues
2,4$ Spores smaller, never exceeding  10 µm ; hymenophoral trama of long elements; clamp-connections frequent in hymenium on1y, elsewhere rare
3,0 $ In (sub-)alpine habitats; spores 7.5-11.5 x 6.5-10 µm , Qm = 1.15, weakly angled, almost rounded $ E. atrosericeum
3,0 $ In xerophytic grassland; spores 7.0-11 x 7.0-8.5 µm , Qm = 1.15, irregularly 6-7-angled $ E. vindobonense
4,0 $ Pileus with surface very dark, more or less waxy, hardly fibrillose; spores with 6-7 angles, more or less indistinctly angled; in montane Pinus forest, sometimes also with Alnus incana $ E. brunneosericeum
4,5 $ Pileus surface pruinose to hoary, especially at centre; spores pronouncedly angled
5,0 $ Pileus with small, persistent umbo, radially fibrillose with pruinose apex; in mixed wood and copses of Buxus and Prunus spinosa $ E. conicosericeum
5,0 $ Pileus depressed or with weak umbo within central depression, entirely pruinose- hoary when fresh; in dune grasslands with Salix repens . $ E. cinereoopacum
6,0 $ Spores (6.8)-7.3-8.4-(9.2) x (5.9)-6.3-7.2-(8)   µm , Xm = 7.8 x 6.8 µm , Q = (1.01)- 1.09-1.23-(1.36), Qm = 1.16; in Mediterranean vegetation .... $ E. minutisporum
6,7$ Spores 7.7-10-(11) µm long
7,0 $ Smell spermatical; spores 8.3-9.6 x 7.5-8.5 µm , Xm = 8.9 x 7.9 µm , Q = 1-1.21, Qm = 1.12, with 5-7 angles in side-view; in Mediterranean Cistus or Quercus vegetation $ E. llimonae
7,8 $ Smell different; spores usually regu1arly 5(-6) angled in side-view; in Mediterranean vegetation (Pinus, Quercus) or not
8,0 $ Smell raphanoid-herbaceous; pileus often conico-campanulate for a long time, very strongly aeriferous-lustrous with loose fibrils when dry; spores with weak angles $ E. nitens
8,9 $ Smell farinaceous; pileus different; spores with more pronounced angles
9,0 $ Basidioma small; pileus slightly sericeous; stipe smooth or rarely with fine fibrils; in Pinus or Quercus forests $ E. juncinum
9,0 $ Basidioma more robust; pileus sericeous; stipe densely to hardly fibrillose; in meadows and pastures, wide-spread in whole of Europe $ E. sericeum

